<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vatican Museums</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1506–</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vatican Museums are the art and archaeological museums of the popes in Vatican City, begun in 1506 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a> displayed the newly found statue of Laocoön. Their collections include classical sculpture, Egyptian and Etruscan antiquities, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a> Rooms and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel">Sistine Chapel</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a>'s ceiling and Last Judgement. They are among the most visited museums in the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vatican-museums.jpg" alt="Vatican Museums" width="320" height="175" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Fabio Mochi Vector: Kaidor · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Musei_vaticani_Coat_of_Arms.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
